In Swift: text.components(separatedBy: "\n---\n") gives a full split. To emulate split
n, use a prefix-limited split. Swift has a trick using `split(separator:maxSplits
:)` on the string: `text.split(separator: "\n---\n", maxSplits: 3, omittingEmptySub
sequences: false)`.

Caveat: String.split's maxSplits is the number of splits, not the number of parts
. maxSplits: 3 gives at most 4 parts. But `split` returns [Substring] and omits empty
 subsequences by default; set omittingEmptySubsequences: false to preserve empties
.

But note: Rust's `str::splitn(4, SEP)` — does it preserve empty strings? Yes, Rust
's splitn preserves empty strings (e.g., "a\n---\n\n---\n1\n---\nb" → ["a", "", "
1", "b"]). With Swift's `split(separator:maxSplits:omittingEmptySubsequences: false
)` we also get empty substrings. Good.

So:

```swift
public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable {
    case malformedFormat
}

public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note {
    let parts = text.split(separator: "\n---\n", maxSplits: 3, omittingEmptySubsequences
: false)
    guard parts.count == 4 else {
        throw NoteParseError.malformedFormat
    }
    return Note(
        id: String(parts[0]),
        title: String(parts[1]),
        pinned: String(parts[2]) == "1",
        body: String(parts[3]),
        tags: extractTags(from: String(parts[3]))
    )
}
```

